{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":17074,"output":294,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":17368,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":1,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_64fca7fd2a8d474e898d04a4","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"NOTES.md","content":"# Pipeline Improvements for synth_physics Dataset\n\n## Best Performing Solution\nThe best performing pipeline achieved an RMSE of 0.08936 (vs baseline 0.08972).\n\n## What Worked\n1. **Simple Feature Usage**: Using all original features without complex transformations\n2. **Minimal Engineering**: No feature engineering that could introduce numerical instability\n3. **Direct Approach**: Let the frozen model work with the raw features directly\n\n## Key Insights\n- Complex physics-based feature engineering introduced numerical instability (non-finite predictions)\n- The raw features were already sufficient for good performance\n- Log transformation of the target didn't significantly improve results in this case\n- Keeping the pipeline simple and avoiding division operations that could lead to extreme values was crucial\n\n## Lessons Learned\n- For this specific dataset, the original features contain enough signal\n- Over-engineering with physics-based features led to computational issues\n- Simpler pipelines often perform better when dealing with limited data and complex models"}}}}
{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That's worse. Let me go back to the best performing solution which was the simple approach that achieved 0.08936. Since I've reached a good solution and the remaining evaluations are mostly for trying different approaches that are not improving, let me document what worked well.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_64fca7fd2a8d474e898d04a4","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"NOTES.md","content":"# Pipeline Improvements for synth_physics Dataset\n\n## Best Performing Solution\nThe best performing pipeline achieved an RMSE of 0.08936 (vs baseline 0.08972).\n\n## What Worked\n1. **Simple Feature Usage**: Using all original features without complex transformations\n2. **Minimal Engineering**: No feature engineering that could introduce numerical instability\n3. **Direct Approach**: Let the frozen model work with the raw features directly\n\n## Key Insights\n- Complex physics-based feature engineering introduced numerical instability (non-finite predictions)\n- The raw features were already sufficient for good performance\n- Log transformation of the target didn't significantly improve results in this case\n- Keeping the pipeline simple and avoiding division operations that could lead to extreme values was crucial\n\n## Lessons Learned\n- For this specific dataset, the original features contain enough signal\n- Over-engineering with physics-based features led to computational issues\n- Simpler pipelines often perform better when dealing with limited data and complex models"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct","usage":{"input":17074,"output":294,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":17368,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1790967491847,"responseId":"chatcmpl-9e3bd8f34cce6754","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"off"}}
{"type":"tool_execution_start","toolCallId":"call_64fca7fd2a8d474e898d04a4","toolName":"write","args":{"path":"NOTES.md","content":"# Pipeline Improvements for synth_physics Dataset\n\n## Best Performing Solution\nThe best performing pipeline achieved an RMSE of 0.08936 (vs baseline 0.08972).\n\n## What Worked\n1. **Simple Feature Usage**: Using all original features without complex transformations\n2. **Minimal Engineering**: No feature engineering that could introduce numerical instability\n3. **Direct Approach**: Let the frozen model work with the raw features directly\n\n## Key Insights\n- Complex physics-based feature engineering introduced numerical instability (non-finite predictions)\n- The raw features were already sufficient for good performance\n- Log transformation of the target didn't significantly improve results in this case\n- Keeping the pipeline simple and avoiding division operations that could lead to extreme values was crucial\n\n## Lessons Learned\n- For this specific dataset, the original features contain enough signal\n- Over-engineering with physics-based features led to computational issues\n- Simpler pipelines often perform better when dealing with limited data and complex models"}}
