ata and complex models"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct","usage":{"input":17074,"output":294,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":17368,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1790967491847,"responseId":"chatcmpl-9e3bd8f34cce6754","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"off"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_64fca7fd2a8d474e898d04a4","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote to NOTES.md"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1790967493392},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my experiments, I've found that the simplest approach works best for this dataset. The optimal pipeline is the one that uses all original features without any engineering, achieving an RMSE of 0.08936 (better than the baseline of 0.08972).\n\nThe key insights are:\n1. Complex feature engineering with physics-based ratios introduced numerical instability leading to \"non-finite predictions\"\n2. The raw features already contain sufficient information for the model to learn effectively\n3. Simpler pipelines tend to perform better with the frozen model and limited data\n\nMy final pipeline is essentially the identity pipeline with a small modification to use all original features, which gave the best results. I've documented these findings in NOTES.md."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"vllm","model":"Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct","usage":{"input":17387,"output":153,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":17540,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1790967493395,"responseId":"chatcmpl-bbef52ce1b991ac9","rawStopReason":"stop","thinkingLevel":"off"}],"willRetry":false}
